    "excerpt": "In their 2014 book The Second Machine Age, academics Erik Brynjolfsson and Andrew McAfee identify the productivity explosion of the nineteenth and twentieth centuries as the First Machine Age. Whereas technological innovations before the Industrial MRevolution were primarily augmentations of an individual’s capacity, the Industrial Revolution created tools that far exceeded their maker. From the synergy of the productive ensemble came such material abundance that a person living after was to a person living before almost as if an entirely new species.",
